  "plain_language": {
    "summary": "ON THIS MODEL AND THESE 154 QUESTIONS the content-addressed write plus read-gate line is now closed. Swapping how a record's address is computed -- from averaging the model's own byte-level context vectors to encoding each position separately, weighting the strongest positions, and subtracting one common offset -- did not buy the two things that must hold together: the 22 written records' own read wordings dropped to 0.77 similarity at the worst (the pass line is 0.90) and only 1 of the 22 landed on its own record, while the three classes of questions that were never written ended up CLOSER to the records than before (gaps -0.18, -0.20, -0.22, all negative). What this shows is not that the threshold, the offset or the particular linear transform was wrong, but that the raw material an address is built from -- the context vectors this model produces for Chinese byte text -- does not yet keep 'same content, different wording' stable while keeping 'never written' apart. No further encoding, geometry or threshold tuning is planned on this line. What it does NOT show: that content addressing cannot work in principle, that the model cannot learn, or that growing the memory would not help. It shows only that on the frozen graph of today, this mechanism has no usable margin.",
    "can_prove": "if the pass rule holds: on this model and these 154 questions, a memory that finds a record by how similar the question is can keep recognising its own paraphrases (every one of the 22 written wordings lands on its own record, with similarity at least 0.90) while three kinds of questions that were never answered -- a word that was never written, a pairing that was never written, and a point between two records -- all stay below the same line. That would make a single 'only answer if similar enough' rule usable on this line.",
    "cannot_prove": "it cannot show that the model has become smarter, learned anything, or kept any old ability: nothing was trained and no answer quality is measured here. It cannot show that the memory still works with longer text, many more records, or across sessions. And it cannot show that growing the memory (more slots) would help; a usable address only means growth would have something to grow for, not that growth pays.",
    "if_failed": "if the pass rule does not hold: on the byte representation this model currently produces, exchanging the way a record's address is computed still cannot separate 'recognise my own wording' from 'know that this was never written', so the content-addressed write + read gate line is closed here. It does not show that content addressing is impossible in principle, because the raw material of an address -- the model's own context vectors -- was not changed in this round."
  },
